because alpha's are few and far between in real world terms  a lot of
companies are not likly to want to make the effort to build an alpha
port of a propritary package.

By being able to run x86 binaries in a reasonable way the alpha becomes
more appealing a platform. When you say well you can't run app A and app
B on the system the first question that people ask is why get the
platform at all.

Weather we like it or not windows is a popluar platform that runs a lot
of software. It would be nice to be able to run windows software on
other platforms. That is exactly why Wine exists. Wine on alpha is just
an extention of the arguement of providing portablity.

Dec developed tools to help port/emulate Solaris binaries. I am sure the
reason was to increase the appeal of the Alpha to Sun users.



